<html>
<title>Assembler</title>
<body>


<h1>Assembler</h1>

<p>
On the command line (1), type in 180x code (SYSTEM00, 1801, 1802, 1804, or 1805, depending on what CPU is running). For command syntax, see the <a href="SyntaxMachineCode.htm">Machine Code</a> section. The syntax is similar to, or the same as, that defined in the 180x data sheets.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_command_line.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Command line</strong> input field</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Working address</strong> indicators</li>
</ul>

<p>
The current working address is shown in bold, between <strong>&gt;</strong> and <strong>&lt;</strong> (2). To change the working address see the <a href="DirectNavigation.htm">Navigation</a> section.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> All data values must be given as hexadecimal values unless preceded by <tt>&amp;</tt>, in which case the values are interpreted as decimal, i.e., <tt>LDI &amp;100</tt> is the same as <tt>LDI 64</tt>.
</p>

<p>
To use the pseudo-code assembler, an emulator must be started that runs one of the pseudo codes listed in the <a href="SyntaxPseudoCode.htm">Pseudo Code</a> section.
</p>

<p>
In addition to 180x and pseudo-code, the options described in the following subsections are available from the command line.
</p>


<h2>Data input</h2>

<p>
<strong>Format:</strong> <tt>#ww xx yy zz</tt>
</p>

<p>
Where ww, xx, yy, zz are hexadecimal values that will be stored at the current and following working addresses. At least one value (ww) must be specified. Spaces after <tt>#</tt> and between bytes are optional.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Example:</strong> type in <tt># 10 20 30</tt> (1) at address 4424; this will result in code 10 20 30 at 4424-4426 (2):
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_hash.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Command line</strong> input</li>
<li>(2) Resulting code at working address</li>
</ul>

<p>
<strong>Other examples:</strong>
</p>
<pre>
# 10       stores value 10 at the current address
# 20 30    stores 20 at the current address and 30 at the following address
</pre>


<h2>ASCII Text</h2>

<p>
<strong>Format:</strong> <tt>'text</tt>
</p>

<p>
Where all text after <tt>'</tt> will be stored in ASCII code at the current working address.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Example:</strong> type in <tt>'SOME TEXT</tt> (1) at address 4427; this will result in ASCII code for SOME TEXT at 4427-442F (2):
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_text.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Command line</strong> input</li>
<li>(2) Resulting ASCII code at working address</li>
</ul>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> When using this on the Studio IV, characters are stored with Studio IV character numbering.
</p>


<h2>ASCII Text - High Colour</h2>

<p>
<strong>Format:</strong> <tt>"text</tt>
</p>

<p>
Only applicable to the COMX to store ASCII text in <strong>high colour</strong>; all text after <tt>"</tt> will be stored in ASCII code (range 80 to FF) at the current working address.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Example:</strong> type in <tt>"SOME TEXT</tt> (1) at address 4430; this will result in ASCII code in <strong>high colour</strong> for SOME TEXT at 4430-4438 (2):
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_high_text.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Command line</strong> input</li>
<li>(2) Resulting high colour ASCII code at working address</li>
</ul>


<h2>Branch Address</h2>

<p>
<strong>Format:</strong> <tt>:hex address</tt>
</p>

<p>
Store 16-bit <strong>Branch data</strong>; see the <a href="DirectBranchData.htm">Branch Data</a> section.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Example:</strong> type in <tt>:1234</tt> (1) at address 4439; this will result in 12 at address 4439 and 34 at address 443A, i.e., address 1234 (2):
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_address.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Command line</strong> input</li>
<li>(2) Resulting branch address at working address</li>
</ul>
</body>
</html>
